WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.700
Groetch: The rice milk
and the coconut milk

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.370
and the almond milk are
very low in protein.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.450
So if you're looking
to get a protein source

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:10.480
like a vegetarian or
plant-based protein source,

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.130
these are not very good.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:15.080
Narrator: That's registered
dietician Marion Groetch.

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:16.840
Today she's gonna help us sort through

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.970
all of these different non-dairy milks

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:21.794
and tell us which ones might
just be better for you.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.290
Narrator: In the last several years,

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:31.420
the market for non-dairy
milks has exploded,

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.610
and that's great for
the some 65% of adults

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:36.550
who are lactose intolerant,

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:38.360
but with more than a dozen varieties

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:40.090
now crowding the dairy aisle,

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.760
it's hard to know which one to choose.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:44.070
Ultimately, Groetch says,

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:46.170
it comes down to what you're looking for.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.310
Groetch: So if you're looking
to get closest to cow's milk,

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:50.260
I would probably go with a soy milk

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:52.040
just because it has more protein.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:54.470
It has about seven
grams of protein per cup

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:57.520
as opposed to eight grams in cow's milk,

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.690
and then oat milk is somewhere in between.

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:03.000
About four grams of protein
per cup of oat milk.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.060
Soy is high in protein
because it comes from a bean,

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.140
whereas oat comes from a grain,

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:10.470
and it tends to have less protein in it.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.350
The protein in the soy
milk is also more complete.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.250
If you're looking to reduce your calories,

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:16.740
almond milk might be a good choice.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.630
So the rice milk here
is higher in calories

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:20.810
or the highest in calories,

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:23.530
and then we have oat, soy, coconut,

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:26.730
and almond milk is usually
the lowest in calories.

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:28.680
Narrator: But if you're trying
to watch your fat intake,

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.390
you might actually want
to avoid coconut milk.

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:34.910
Groetch: So soy has about
four grams of fat per cup,

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:36.830
and again, these are healthier fats

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:39.010
than the fats that you
might find in a 2% milk,

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:42.150
but it's equivalent in the amount of fat.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.450
Oat milk, about three grams.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:46.070
It depends on the brand.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:47.500
Coconut milk is a little bit higher.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:48.940
It's about six grams of fat.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:51.510
I don't really see the
benefit to coconut milk.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.330
It's higher in saturated fats,

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:57.190
although now we know that
the saturated fat in coconut

00:01:57.190 --> 00:02:00.440
is probably not as detrimental
to cardiovascular health

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:01.830
as we once thought,

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.310
but I think the jury is still out,

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:07.520
and I wouldn't be taking
a lot of saturated fats

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.363
from coconut products right now.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.310
Narrator: But when you're
choosing which milk to buy,

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:13.500
there's more to consider than just

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.360
protein, calories, and fat.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.830
Groetch: Most of them are fortified with

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:20.770
vitamin D, vitamin A, vitamin B12,

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:23.370
but you have to purchase a fortified milk.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:26.250
So some are fortifying up to 45%

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.930
of the daily value for calcium,

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:31.360
whereas others are only
fortifying up to 10%.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:34.620
So this rice milk is fortified
with 30% of the daily value.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.620
So the daily value for
calcium is 1,000 milligrams,

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.570
and that covers most people.

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:43.460
So 30% per cup would mean 300
milligrams of calcium per cup,

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.200
and that's equivalent to cow's milk.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.780
Coconut milk, actually,
is only fortified at 10%.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:50.770
So you'd only get 100
milligrams of calcium per cup.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.520
So it would be difficult
to meet your calcium needs

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:56.023
if you're relying on a
product like this for calcium.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:58.960
They're mostly not fortified

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:00.820
with other nutrients like potassium.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:03.370
Soy milk, actually, is a
good source of potassium,

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:05.650
but some of the others are not.

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:07.960
So it really just depends on the milk

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:09.060
and how it's fortified.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.048
It depends on the brand.

00:03:11.048 --> 00:03:12.190
Narrator: And that's just it.

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:14.715
Nutrients don't just vary by type of milk,

00:03:14.715 --> 00:03:16.147
like almond or soy,

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:19.200
but by brand, like Blue Diamond or Silk,

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.380
and so do calories and fat.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:24.730
So the best way to choose
the right milk is simple.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:25.863
Check out the label.

